    description: "Augmenting stack frames with prefix minima pairs (LIFO with Running Extremum State) \u2014 Time: O(1) Push, O(1) Pop, O(1) Top, O(1) getMin.",
    theory: "#### Problem Definition & Motivation\nA standard stack is a Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) container supporting two primary operations:\n* `push(x)`: Appends an element to the top of the stack.\n* `pop()`: Removes the most recently added element from the top of the stack.\n\nIn many algorithmic contexts, we also need to continuously query the smallest element currently residing anywhere inside the stack:\n$$\\min_{0 \\le j \\le \\text{size}-1} \\text{stack}[j]$$\nIn an unmodified stack, computing this value requires either iterating through all $N$ elements (costing $O(N)$ time per query) or maintaining an external sorted container like a balanced binary search tree or priority queue (which degrades push and pop times to $O(\\log N)$).\n\nOur goal is to modify the stack data structure in such a way that finding the smallest element in the stack takes strictly $O(1)$ constant time, while preserving $O(1)$ push and pop operations without any logarithmic overhead.\n\n#### Mathematical Invariant & Pair Decomposition\nOn a stack, elements are added and removed only at one end (the top). Because of this strict LIFO discipline, the set of elements located underneath any element $x$ remains completely static throughout the entire lifespan of $x$ in the stack.\n\nTherefore, for every element added to the stack at height $i$, we do not merely store the raw element itself. Instead, we store a pair:\n$$\\text{st}[i] = \\langle \\text{element}_i, \\ \\mu_i \\rangle$$\nwhere $\\mu_i$ represents the minimum value among all elements from the bottom of the stack up to index $i$:\n$$\\mu_i = \\min_{0 \\le j \\le i} \\text{element}_j$$\n\n* **Base Case (Empty Stack)**:\n  When pushing onto an empty stack, the new element $x$ is trivially the minimum of the stack:\n  $$\\mu_0 = x$$\n* **Inductive Step (Non-Empty Stack)**:\n  When pushing a new element $x$ onto a non-empty stack with top minimum $\\mu_{\\text{top}}$, the new running minimum is:\n  $$\\mu_{\\text{new}} = \\min(x, \\mu_{\\text{top}})$$\n* **Constant Time Retrieval**:\n  To obtain the minimum of the entire stack at any given moment, we simply read the second component of the pair located at the top of the stack:\n  $$\\text{minimum} = \\text{st.top().second}$$\n* **Pop Invariance**:\n  When removing an element via `pop()`, the top pair is discarded. The new top pair already contains the precalculated minimum of all remaining elements below it. Hence, the previous state is automatically restored in $O(1)$ time without requiring recalculation.\n\n#### ASCII Architecture & State Transitions\n```text\nState 1: push(5)              State 2: push(2)                  State 3: push(7)\n+-----------------------+     +-----------------------+         +-----------------------+\n| (5, min(5)=5)   [TOP] |     | (2, min(2,5)=2) [TOP] |         | (7, min(7,2)=2) [TOP] |\n+-----------------------+     +-----------------------+         +-----------------------+\n                              | (5, 5)                |         | (2, 2)                |\n                              +-----------------------+         +-----------------------+\n                                                                | (5, 5)                |\n                                                                +-----------------------+\ngetMin() -> 5                 getMin() -> 2                     getMin() -> 2\n\nState 4: pop() -> removes (7, 2)\n+-----------------------+\n| (2, 2)          [TOP] |  --> getMin() immediately inspects top.second = 2 in O(1)!\n+-----------------------+\n| (5, 5)                |\n+-----------------------+\n```\n\n#### Complexity & Memory Analysis\n* **Time Complexity**:\n  * `push(x)`: $O(1)$ \u2014 Performs at most one `min()` comparison and one push.\n  * `pop()`: $O(1)$ \u2014 Discards the top pair in constant time.\n  * `top()`: $O(1)$ \u2014 Inspects `st.top().first`.\n  * `getMin()`: $O(1)$ \u2014 Inspects `st.top().second`.\n* **Space Complexity**:\n  * $O(N)$ auxiliary memory \u2014 Each frame stores an integer value and an integer minimum.",
    code: "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass MinStack {\nprivate:\n    // Storing pairs: {element, minimum from bottom up to this element}\n    stack<pair<int, int>> st;\n\npublic:\n    MinStack() {}\n\n    // 1. Adding an element (Push):\n    void push(int new_elem) {\n        int new_min = st.empty() ? new_elem : min(new_elem, st.top().second);\n        st.push({new_elem, new_min});\n    }\n\n    // 2. Removing an element (Pop):\n    void pop() {\n        if (!st.empty()) {\n            st.pop();\n        }\n    }\n\n    // 3. Inspecting the top element:\n    int top() {\n        return st.top().first;\n    }\n\n    // 4. Finding the minimum in O(1):\n    int getMin() {\n        return st.top().second;\n    }\n\n    // 5. Checking if the stack is empty:\n    bool empty() {\n        return st.empty();\n    }\n};",
